		{
			"id": "TVtqD1EJEUJ_GsDLx",
			"name": "Mark of Nurgle",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Nurgle, the Plague God, gives his chosen a gift that looks like a curse: disease that does not kill, flesh that rots without failing, and a cheerful stubbornness against pain. Codex: Chaos Space Marines background describes Death Guard and Plague Marines as slow, bloated and almost impossible to put down, trailing flies and stench. Wounds that would drop a living warrior leave them shambling on.\n\nDesign: Framework Marks table. Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (Powers p.53, 25/level, level = divisor) divisor 2 = 50; Immunity to disease (B81) 10; Resistant to poison +8 (B81), Occasional hazard 10 x1/2 = 5 (the same figure the Astartes Preomnor uses); Hard to Kill 2 (B58) 4; Hideous Appearance (B21) -16; Odious Personal Habit (B22) -5 x 2 levels = -10 (a smell and a swarm of flies together are a moderate-to-serious habit). 50 + 10 + 5 + 4 - 16 - 10 = 43 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Physical",
				"Chaos",
				"Nurgle",
				"Mark of Chaos"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "toR5xYyPzYIu6uas7",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 2 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 2 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated). Nurgle's Gift adds natural DR, not another level, so a Plague Marine's total divisor stays 2.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tZtv0LFt7KqgMtiGW",
					"name": "Immunity to Disease",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Immune to disease, including most warp-spawned plagues. Not to Nurgle's own Rot when wielded as a weapon effect, if the GM wants it dangerous.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tJ9_iDM-wqRO701ji",
					"name": "Resistant to Poison",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "+8 to HT rolls to resist poison.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 7
				},
				{
					"id": "t1heysY84Ks50TeaU",
					"name": "Hard to Kill",
					"reference": "B58",
					"local_notes": "+2 to HT rolls made solely to avoid death.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical"
					],
					"points_per_level": 2,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				},
				{
					"id": "tzeurxE0iKmlg_Rif",
					"name": "Hideous Appearance",
					"reference": "B21",
					"local_notes": "Bloated, weeping and rotted; -4 reaction from anyone not sworn to Nurgle.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Physical",
						"Social"
					],
					"base_points": -16
				},
				{
					"id": "t0F8LvbdALA2D2YAw",
					"name": "Odious Personal Habit (Rot and Flies)",
					"reference": "B22",
					"local_notes": "A cloud of flies and the smell of decay. Stealth is not an option; -2 reaction from those who smell it.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Physical",
						"Social"
					],
					"points_per_level": -5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TaQhw8L6bFLZtGWKX",
			"name": "Mark of Tzeentch",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Tzeentch, the Changer of Ways, gives his servants sorcery, schemes and a shimmering ward against harm. Codex: Chaos Space Marines background makes the Thousand Sons and every Tzeentchian warband psychic by habit and conspiratorial by nature; the god's plans fold into one another until nobody, the servant included, can say what is true.\n\nDesign: Framework Marks table. Psyker (Talent) +1 (10/level) via the project's existing Psyker trait. Warding field Shield Points 20 priced at 0.5 point per SP, the rate the project's Zoanthrope warp field uses (200 SP for 100 points): 10 points. Paranoia (B148) -10; Compulsive Lying (B128) -15, self-control 12. 10 + 10 - 10 - 15 = -5 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Chaos",
				"Tzeentch",
				"Mark of Chaos"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tUosPuCYAHWtNtG4M",
					"name": "Psyker",
					"reference": "TSOR5",
					"local_notes": "This project's Sorcery Talent (Thaumatology: Sorcery, TSOR5) analogue:\napplies as Sorcery Talent applies to a sorcerer's skills in TSOR, just\nto a 40k-specific list — Psyniscience, Meditation, Hidden Lore (the\nWarp), and the Will roll to resist Perils of the Warp (see above) — a\nhouse extension of Talent's normal \"add to listed skills\" rule,\nprecedented by Magery's and Sorcery Talent's own habit of aiding\nrelated rolls beyond pure spellcasting. Reaction bonus: +1/level from\nother sanctioned psykers and the Astra Telepathica.\n\nPsyker (Talent) and Warp Empowerment are separate purchases on separate\naxes, exactly as Sorcery Talent and Sorcerous Empowerment are in TSOR:\nthis trait only sharpens related skills and Perils resistance, while\nWarp Empowerment (above) is what actually gates which powers a psyker\nmay know. A high-Talent, low-Empowerment psyker is a sharp, well-drilled\nmind who simply hasn't learned much yet; the reverse is a raw prodigy\nwho is clumsy with the skills around their gift.\n\nImperial Assignment scale (Adeptus Astra Telepathica / Black Ships),\nweakest to strongest, mapped to Talent level. See Warp Empowerment above\nfor the matching Empowerment level/point-cap column, which is the real\ngate on ability selection:\n  Iota    — Talent 0 (latent; register but rarely take Psyker traits at all)\n  Zeta    — Talent 1\n  Eta     — Talent 1\n  Theta   — Talent 2\n  Epsilon — Talent 2  (typical Wyrdvane conscript)\n  Delta   — Talent 3  (typical Sanctioned Primaris Psyker)\n  Gamma   — Talent 4\n  Beta    — Talent 4  (many Astropaths)\n  Alpha   — Talent 5  (Astartes Librarians, Black Ship candidates)\n  Alpha-Plus — Talent 6+ (beyond-play tier: Primarchs,\n    the most powerful Inquisitors, the worst Chaos sorcerers)\n\nLore: The Iota-to-Alpha-Plus assignment scale is drawn from the Adeptus Astra Telepathica's own grading of psychic potential as depicted across Dark Heresy-line material and repeated in Codex/Imperial Armour background; Black Library uses the same grades in passing (e.g. Eisenhorn-cycle and Ciaphas Cain references to Alpha-class talents). \"Alpha-Plus\" specifically denotes the rarest, most dangerous talents the Black Ships process. Grade names beyond Alpha/Beta/Gamma are RPG-line material only.\n\nDesign: Talent affecting four things (three skills plus the Perils resistance roll) sits at Basic's \"Medium Talent\" rate of 10 points/level (B90, three-to-five-skill bracket) rather than 5 (one-two skills) or 15 (six-nine skills) — the same rate Sorcery Talent itself uses (TSOR5, 10/level). Capped at level 6 for player characters; higher grades are GM-reference only, per the framework's \"Beyond play\" tier.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Talent",
						"Psyker",
						"Imperium"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1,
					"base_points": 0,
					"max_levels": 6
				},
				{
					"id": "t8BPujNsHo-akaBL4",
					"name": "Warding Field (Tzeentch)",
					"local_notes": "Shield Points 20, as the project's personal force-field rules (framework.md, \"Personal force fields\"): damage from an attack the field covers comes off SP first and armour divisors do not apply to SP. If an attack does more than the SP left, the field drops to 0 and the excess carries on to worn armour at the attack's full divisor; a bolt's follow-up detonates on the field and also comes off SP. Covers all attacks. Recharge: after 3 seconds with no damage it regains 10 SP per second; a field knocked to 0 waits double that delay. Track current SP with a counter.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tRFqOdtCooEhxSEEj",
					"name": "Paranoia",
					"reference": "B148",
					"local_notes": "Sees plots in everything; the schemes of Tzeentch are everywhere.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				},
				{
					"id": "tRhHYu9T369KK-2N5",
					"name": "Compulsive Lying",
					"reference": "B128",
					"local_notes": "Self-control 12. Lies when the truth would serve as well.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental"
					],
					"base_points": -15,
					"cr": 12
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T6lqHnnoIImeaSR4N",
			"name": "Mark of Slaanesh",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Slaanesh, the Prince of Pleasure, drives her followers toward ever stronger sensation. Codex: Chaos Space Marines background describes the Emperor's Children and the Noise Marines as warriors whose senses have been sharpened until a bolter's roar is music and a wound is an experience. They are fast, perceptive and unable to stop chasing the next feeling.\n\nDesign: Framework Marks table. Combat Reflexes (B43) 15, disabled by default for the reason in its notes; Enhanced Move (Ground) 1/2 (B52) 10; acute senses taken as Hearing, Touch and Taste/Smell at +2 each (B35, 2/level) = 12; Compulsive Behavior (B128) -10; Sadism (B152) -15. Total with the defaults: 10 + 12 - 10 - 15 = -3; enabling Combat Reflexes makes it 12. Acute Vision is left out: the Astartes Occulobe already covers it.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Physical",
				"Mental",
				"Chaos",
				"Slaanesh",
				"Mark of Chaos"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
